WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:08.327
"You can't ask the jury for a verdict," the White Rabbit explained to the King.

2
00:00:08.328 --> 00:00:11.660
"There's more evidence to come."

3
00:00:11.661 --> 00:00:16.495
The White Rabbit bent over and picked up a folded piece of paper.

4
00:00:16.496 --> 00:00:20.910
"See? This item has just been found on the floor."

5
00:00:20.911 --> 00:00:26.119
The Queen leaned forward to get a better look. "What is it?"

6
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:30.095
"I haven't opened it yet," the White Rabbit said.

7
00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:36.056
"Perhaps it's a letter written by the Knave to . . . to somebody."

8
00:00:36.057 --> 00:00:41.914
"Unless it was written to nobody," the King said.

9
00:00:41.915 --> 00:00:46.878
"Although that would be most unusual."

10
00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:50.409
All the jurors nodded in agreement.

11
00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:55.226
"To whom is it addressed?" one juror asked.

12
00:00:55.227 --> 00:00:57.943
"It's not addressed to anyone."

13
00:00:57.944 --> 00:01:01.607
The White Rabbit unfolded the paper as he spoke.

14
00:01:01.608 --> 00:01:06.829
"In fact, there's nothing written on the outside.

15
00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:15.031
Oh, it isn't a letter after all. It's a poem."

16
00:01:15.032 --> 00:01:20.759
"Is it written in the prisoner's handwriting?" another juror asked.

17
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:27.053
"No, it's not, and I find that very strange," the White Rabbit said.

18
00:01:27.054 --> 00:01:36.698
"The Knave must have imitated someone else's handwriting," the King said.

19
00:01:36.699 --> 00:01:43.179
The Knave sighed. "I didn't write the poem, Your Majesty.

20
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.471
And no one can prove that I did.

21
00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:48.962
Is there a name signed at the end of the poem?"

22
00:01:48.963 --> 00:01:53.908
After carefully reading the poem, the White Rabbit shook his head.

23
00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:56.598
"No, there's no name anywhere."

24
00:01:56.599 --> 00:01:59.406
But the King was not satisfied.

25
00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:01.388
He spoke to the Knave.

26
00:02:01.389 --> 00:02:08.245
"You didn't sign the poem, which only makes things worse.

27
00:02:08.246 --> 00:02:11.963
You were obviously up to some mischief.

28
00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:21.579
Otherwise, you would have signed your name like an honest man."

29
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.993
Everyone in the courtroom clapped wildly.

30
00:02:25.994 --> 00:02:33.643
"That's the first clever thing the King has said all day!" one juror said to another.

31
00:02:33.644 --> 00:02:40.615
"Be quiet!" the Queen said, and everyone settled down.

32
00:02:40.616 --> 00:02:47.601
"If the Knave didn't sign the poem, that proves his guilt, so off with—"

33
00:02:47.602 --> 00:02:52.532
"No! That doesn't prove anything at all," Alice interrupted.

34
00:02:52.533 --> 00:02:57.538
"You don't even know what the poem is about."

35
00:02:57.539 --> 00:03:02.433
"Read the poem!" the King commanded the White Rabbit.

36
00:03:02.434 --> 00:03:05.774
"Where shall I begin, Your Majesty?"

37
00:03:05.775 --> 00:03:09.882
"Begin at the beginning," the King said.

38
00:03:09.883 --> 00:03:15.592
"And keep reading aloud until you get to the end."

39
00:03:15.593 --> 00:03:19.837
And that is exactly what the White Rabbit did.

40
00:03:19.838 --> 00:03:24.573
There was dead silence in the courtroom as he read the poem.

41
00:03:24.574 --> 00:03:28.952
"This is the most ridiculous poem I've ever heard," Alice thought.

42
00:03:28.953 --> 00:03:31.404
"It makes no sense at all."

43
00:03:31.405 --> 00:03:34.533
But the King had another opinion.

44
00:03:34.534 --> 00:03:40.142
"This is the most important evidence we've heard all day."

45
00:03:40.143 --> 00:03:42.499
He rubbed his hands together.

46
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:47.554
"Now the jury must decide—"

47
00:03:47.555 --> 00:03:52.694
"If any of them can explain it, I'll give them some money," Alice said.

48
00:03:52.695 --> 00:03:55.352
"I don't think that poem means anything."

49
00:03:55.353 --> 00:04:01.225
She had grown so large that she wasn't afraid to interrupt the King.

50
00:04:01.226 --> 00:04:03.693
The King ignored her.

51
00:04:03.694 --> 00:04:10.255
"Jury, what is your verdict?" he said for about the 20th time.

52
00:04:10.256 --> 00:04:19.233
"No, no!" the Queen said. "Tell us the prison sentence first, then the verdict."

53
00:04:19.234 --> 00:04:22.964
"That's absurd!" Alice said loudly.

54
00:04:22.965 --> 00:04:25.958
"No one gives the prison sentence first.

55
00:04:25.959 --> 00:04:28.752
What if the jury finds the Knave not guilty?

56
00:04:28.753 --> 00:04:33.298
Then there wouldn't even be a sentence!"

57
00:04:33.299 --> 00:04:39.004
"Be quiet!" the Queen said, turning purple with rage.

58
00:04:39.005 --> 00:04:42.760
"I won't!" Alice said.

59
00:04:42.761 --> 00:04:49.678
"Off with her head!" the Queen shouted at the top of her lungs.

60
00:04:49.679 --> 00:04:52.529
Nobody moved.

61
00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:56.298
"Who cares what you think?" Alice said.

62
00:04:56.299 --> 00:05:00.704
By this time, she had grown back to her full size.

63
00:05:00.705 --> 00:05:07.428
"You're nothing but a pack of cards!"

64
00:05:07.429 --> 00:05:15.665
And at this, the whole pack rose into the air and flew at Alice.

